h the p53 family member p63 in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC). ACTL6A and p63 physically interact and cooperatively control a transcriptional program that promotes proliferation and suppresses differentiation, in part through activation of the Hippo-YAP pathway via regulators including WWC1.  Consequently, loss of ACTL6A or p63 in tumor cells induces YAP phosphorylation and inactivation, associated with growth arrest and terminal differentiation, all phenocopied by WWC1 overexpression. In vivo, ectopic ACTLC6A/p63 expression promotes tumorigenesis, while ACTL6A expression and YAP activation are highly correlated in primary HNSCC and predict poor patient survival. Thus, ACTL6A and p63 collaborate as oncogenic drivers in HNSCC. Gene expression profiling of HNSCC cells with and without ablated endogenous p63 via lentiviral shRNA","gpl":"6244","gse":"88833","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2016/11/18","suppfile":"CEL","samples":[{"accession":"GSM2349899","title":"JHU029_shCtrl_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2349908","title":"FaDU_shTP63_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2349905","title":"JHU029_shTP63_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2349902","title":"FaDU_shCtrl_new_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2349904","title":"FaDU_shCtrl_rep4"},{"accession":"GSM2349901","title":"JHU029_shCtrl_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2349910","title":"FaDU_shTP63_rep4"},{"accession":"GSM2349909","title":"FaDU_shTP63_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2349907","title":"JHU029_shTP63_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2349906","title":"JHU029_shTP63_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2349900","title":"JHU029_shCtrl_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2349903","title":"FaDU_shCtrl_new_rep3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["28041841"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE88nnn/GSE88833/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA348822"},"200088832":{"uid":"200088832","accession":"GSE88832","gds":"","title":"Microarray Samples for shTP63 in Immortalized HFK to Investigate How ACTL6A and p63 Activate Hippo-YAP in SCC","summary":"Loss-of-function mutations in SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling subunit genes are observed in many cancers, but an oncogenic role for SWI/SNF is not well established. Here we reveal that ACTL6A, encoding a SWI/SNF subunit linked to stem and progenitor cell function, is frequently co-amplified and highly expressed together with the p53 family member p63 in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC). ACTL6A and p63 physically interact and cooperatively control a transcriptional program that promotes proliferation and suppresses differentiation, in part through activation of the Hippo-YAP pathway via regulators including WWC1.  Consequently, loss of ACTL6A or p63 in tumor cells induces YAP phosphorylation and inactivation, associated with growth arrest and terminal differentiation, all phenocopied by WWC1 overexpression. In vivo, ectopic ACTLC6A/p63 expression promotes tumorigenesis, while ACTL6A expression and YAP activation are highly correlated in primary HNSCC and predict poor patient survival. Thus, ACTL6A and p63 collaborate as oncogenic drivers in HNSCC. Gene expression profiling of untransformed keratinocytes (HaCaT) with and without ablated endogenous p63 via lentiviral shRNA.","gpl":"6244","gse":"88832","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2016/11/18","suppfile":"CEL","samples":[{"accession":"GSM2349895","title":"HaCaT_shCtrl_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2349898","title":"HaCaT_shTP63_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2349897","title":"HaCaT_shTP63_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2349894","title":"HaCaT_shCtrl_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2349896","title":"HaCaT_shTP63_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2349893","title":"HaCaT_shCtrl_rep1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["28041841"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE88nnn/GSE88832/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA348823"},"200088831":{"uid":"200088831","accession":"GSE88831","gds":"","title":"Microarray Samples for shACTL6A in HNSCC Cells to Investigate How ACTL6A and p63 Activate Hippo-YAP in SCC","summary":"Loss-of-function mutations in SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling subunit genes are observed in many cancers, but an oncogenic role for SWI/SNF is not well established. Here we reveal that ACTL6A, encoding a SWI/SNF subunit linked to stem and progenitor cell function, is frequently co-amplified and highly expressed together with the p53 family member p63 in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC). ACTL6A and p63 physically interact and cooperatively control a transcriptional program that promotes proliferation and suppresses differentiation, in part through activation of the Hippo-YAP pathway via regulators including WWC1.  Consequently, loss of ACTL6A or p63 in tumor cells induces YAP phosphorylation and inactivation, associated with growth arrest and terminal differentiation, all phenocopied by WWC1 overexpression. In vivo, ectopic ACTLC6A/p63 expression promotes tumorigenesis, while ACTL6A expression and YAP activation are highly correlated in primary HNSCC and predict poor patient survival. Thus, ACTL6A and p63 collaborate as oncogenic drivers in HNSCC. Gene expression profiling of HNSCC cells with and without ablated endogenous ACTL6A via lentiviral shRNA.","gpl":"6244","gse":"88831","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2016/11/18","suppfile":"CEL","samples":[{"accession":"GSM2349885","title":"FaDU_shCtrl_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2349888","title":"FaDU_shACTL6A_A07_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2349891","title":"FaDU_shACTL6A_B02_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM2349890","title":"FaDU_shACTL6A_B02_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2349884","title":"FaDU_shCtrl_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2349887","title":"FaDU_shACTL6A_A07_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM2349886","title":"FaDU_shCtrl_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2349889","title":"FaDU_shACTL6A_A07_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM2349892","title":"FaDU_shACTL6A_B02_rep3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":9,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["28041841"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE88nnn/GSE88831/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA348824"},"200084713":{"uid":"200084713","accession":"GSE84713","gds":"","title":"Gene expression data from head and neck cancer patient derived xenografts","summary":"Head and neck cancer is a hetergeneous disease. Based on previoulsy defined molecular subtypes we associated gene expression with response to different compounds. We used microarry gene expression for molecular subtyping Cetuximab is the single targeted therapy approved for the treatment of head and neck cancer (SCCHN). Predictive biomarkers have not been established in the clinical setting and patient stratification based on molecular tumor profiles has not been possible. Since EGFR pathway activation is pronounced in basal subtype, we hypothesized this activation could be a predictive signature for an EGFR directed treatment. From our patient derived xenograft platform of head and neck cancer, 28 models were subjected to Affymetrix gene expression studies on HG U133+ 2.0. Based on the expression of 821 genes, the subtype of each of the 28 models was determined by integrating gene expression profiles through centroid-clustering with previously published gene expression data by Keck et al.,CCR 2015. Response was evaluated by comparing tumor volume at treatment initiation and after three weeks of treatment (RTV).","gpl":"570","gse":"84713","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2016/10/01","suppfile":"CEL","samples":[{"accession":"GSM2248212","title":"Sample S12"},{"accession":"GSM2248215","title":"Sample S16"},{"accession":"GSM2248209","title":"Sample S9"},{"accession":"GSM2248223","title":"Sample S24"},{"accession":"GSM2248203","title":"Sample S3"},{"accession":"GSM2248226","title":"Sample S27"},{"accession":"GSM2248206","title":"Sample S6"},{"accession":"GSM2248204","title":"Sample S4"},{"accession":"GSM2248207","title":"Sample S7"},{"accession":"GSM2248224","title":"Sample S25"},{"accession":"GSM2248210","title":"Sample S10"},{"accession":"GSM2248227","title":"Sample S28"},{"accession":"GSM2248221","title":"Sample S22"},{"accession":"GSM2248201","title":"Sample S1"},{"accession":"GSM2248218","title":"Sample S19"},{"accession":"GSM2248202","title":"Sample S2"},{"accession":"GSM2248205","title":"Sample S5"},{"accession":"GSM2248222","title":"Sample S23"},{"accession":"GSM2248225","title":"Sample S26"},{"accession":"GSM2248220","title":"Sample S21"},{"accession":"GSM2248214","title":"Sample S15"},{"accession":"GSM2248219","title":"Sample S20"},{"accession":"GSM2248217","title":"Sample S18"},{"accession":"GSM2248213","title":"Sample S13"},{"accession":"GSM2248211","title":"Sample S11"},{"accession":"GSM2248216","title":"Sample S17"},{"accession":"GSM2248228","title":"Sample S29"},{"accession":"GSM2248208","title":"Sample S8"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":28,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["28560858"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE84nnn/GSE84713/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA330908"},"200072536":{"uid":"200072536","accession":"GSE72536","gds":"","title":"Gene expression analysis of TIL rich HPV driven head and neck tumours reveals a distinct B-cell signature when compared to HPV independent tumours.","summary":"Purpose: Human papilloma virus (HPV) associated head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) has a better prognosis than HPV(-) negative cancer. This may be due, in part, to the higher number of tumour infiltrating lymphocytes (TIL) in HPV(+) tumours. We used RNAseq to evaluate whether these differences in clinical behaviour could be explained simply by a numerical difference in TILs or whether there was a fundamental difference between TILs in these two settings. Patients and methods: Twenty-three consecutive HNSCC cases with high and moderate TIL density were subjected to RNAseq analysis.  Differentially expressed genes (DEG) between 10 HPV(+) and 13 HPV(-) tumours were identified with EdgeR. Immune subset analysis was performed using, FAIME (Functional Analysis of Individual Microarray Expression) and Immune gene transcript count analysis. Results: 1634 genes were differentially expressed. There was a dominant immune signature in HPV(+) tumours. After normalizing expression profiles for numerical differences in T cells and B cells, 437 significantly DEGs still remained. A B-cell associated signature emerged, which segregated HPV(+) from HPV(-) cancers  and included CD200, STAG3, GGA2, SPIB and ADAM28. Differential expression of these genes was confirmed  by real-time quantitative PCR and immunohistochemistry. Conclusion: In our dataset, the difference associated with T-cells between patients with HPV(+) and (-) HNSCC was predominantly numerical. However, when TIL numbers are corrected, a distinct differential B-cell signature was revealed.","gpl":"11154","gse":"72536","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2016/09/01","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM1864257","title":"Patient13"},{"accession":"GSM1864263","title":"Patient19"},{"accession":"GSM1864260","title":"Patient16"},{"accession":"GSM1864246","title":"Patient2"},{"accession":"GSM1864266","title":"Patient22"},{"accession":"GSM1864249","tit